.TH BTRCTL 1 "October 2026" "btrctl" "User Commands"
.
.SH NAME
btrctl \- Btrfs snapshot and trunk backup tool
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B btrctl snapshot
.RB [ \-t | \-\-tag
.IR TAG ...]\&
.RB [ \-H | \-\-host
.IR HOST ]
.br
.B btrctl list
.RB [ \-H | \-\-host
.IR HOST ]
.br
.B btrctl prune
.RB [ \-H | \-\-host
.IR HOST ]
.RB [ \-n | \-\-dry\-run ]
.br
.B btrctl latest
.RB [ \-H | \-\-host
.IR HOST ]
.br
.B btrctl rm
.I SET_NAME
.RB [ \-H | \-\-host
.IR HOST ]
.br
.B btrctl tag
.I SET_NAME
.RB { add | rm }
.IR TAG ...\&
.RB [ \-H | \-\-host
.IR HOST ]
.PP
.B btrctl trunk id
.br
.B btrctl trunk backup
.RI [ SET_NAME ]
.RB [ \-H | \-\-host
.IR HOST ]
.br
.B btrctl trunk hosts
.br
.B btrctl trunk list
.RI [ HOST ]
.br
.B btrctl trunk prune
.RI { HOST | \fB\-a\fR | \fB\-\-all\fR }
.RB [ \-n | \-\-dry\-run ]
.br
.B btrctl trunk rm
.IR HOST / SET_NAME
.br
.B btrctl trunk tag
.IR HOST / SET_NAME
.RB { add | rm }
.IR TAG ...
.PP
.B btrctl config
.br
.B btrctl
.RB { help | \-h | \-\-help }
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B btrctl
takes read-only snapshots of a set of btrfs subvolumes and backs them up,
using
.BR btrfs-send (8)
and
.BR btrfs-receive (8),
to a separately mounted btrfs device called the
.IR trunk .
.PP
A
.I snapshot set
is a directory named after the time it was taken, in the form
.BR YYYY\-MM\-DD\-HHMMSS ,
under
.IR BTRFS_MOUNT_POINT /@snapshots.
It contains one read-only snapshot per configured subvolume, named
.BI \%@snapshot\- TIMESTAMP \- HOST_LABEL \- SUBVOLUME\fR,
and an optional
.B .btrctl
directory holding set metadata such as tags.
Two kinds of symlink live beside the sets:
.B latest
points at the most recently created set, and each
.BI trunk_ ID
points at the set most recently backed up to the trunk device with that
identifier.
The
.B trunk_
links let later backups be sent incrementally.
.PP
On the trunk, each source host has its own subvolume,
.IR TRUNK_MOUNT_POINT /@ HOST_LABEL ,
marked with an empty
.B .btrctl
file so that btrctl-managed subvolumes can be told apart from anything else
on the device.
Received sets are stored under it using the same directory layout as the
source.
.PP
Most commands need root and re-execute themselves through
.BR sudo (8)
when necessary.
Commands that accept
.B \-\-host
run the same btrctl command on a remote machine over
.BR ssh (1);
the connection is made as the invoking (pre-sudo) user, and the remote side
prompts for its own sudo password.
.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Local snapshot sets
.TP
.B snapshot \fR[\fB\-t\fR|\fB\-\-tag\fR \fITAG\fR ...] [\fB\-H\fR|\fB\-\-host\fR \fIHOST\fR]
Take a snapshot set of all configured subvolumes under
.IR BTRFS_MOUNT_POINT ,
then point
.B latest
at it.
Hooks may run before and after; see
.BR HOOKS .
.RS
.TP
.B \-t\fR, \fB\-\-tag \fITAG
Attach a tag to the new set.
Repeat for multiple tags.
.TP
.B \-H\fR, \fB\-\-host \fIHOST
Run on
.I HOST
instead of the local machine.
.RE
.TP
.B list \fR[\fB\-H\fR|\fB\-\-host\fR \fIHOST\fR]
List snapshot sets as a table of host label, set name, tags, and flags.
Flags are
.B latest
and any
.BI trunk_ ID
whose symlink points at the set.
.TP
.B prune \fR[\fB\-H\fR|\fB\-\-host\fR \fIHOST\fR] [\fB\-n\fR|\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
Delete snapshot sets that fall outside the retention policy (see
.BR "RETENTION POLICY" ).
Sets pointed to by
.B latest
or any
.BI trunk_ ID
symlink, and sets tagged
.BR noprune ,
are always kept.
The plan is printed as a table, then a single confirmation is requested for
the whole batch.
Each set's protections are re-checked immediately before it is deleted, and
the command stops at the first deletion failure.
.RS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Print the full plan, including kept sets and the reason each is kept, without
deleting anything or prompting.
.RE
.TP
.B latest \fR[\fB\-H\fR|\fB\-\-host\fR \fIHOST\fR]
Print the name of the set that
.B latest
points to, and nothing else.
.TP
.B rm \fISET_NAME\fR [\fB\-H\fR|\fB\-\-host\fR \fIHOST\fR]
Delete one snapshot set after confirmation.
A warning is printed first if the set is the current
.B latest
or is tracked by any
.BI trunk_ ID
link.
If
.B latest
pointed at the removed set, it is moved to the next most recent set.
.TP
.B tag \fISET_NAME\fR {\fBadd\fR|\fBrm\fR} \fITAG\fR... [\fB\-H\fR|\fB\-\-host\fR \fIHOST\fR]
Add or remove tags on an existing set.
Other tags are preserved; adding a tag that is already present or removing one
that is absent succeeds without change.
Removing the final tag removes the tags file.
.IP
Tags are case-sensitive, must be non-empty, may not begin with
.RB \(lq \- \(rq,
and may not contain commas or newlines.
They are stored one per line in
.IR SET_DIR /.btrctl/tags,
de-duplicated, in insertion order.
The tag
.B noprune
is special: see
.BR prune .
.
.SS Trunk
These commands always run on the machine the trunk device is attached to and
never make an SSH connection.
Where they take a
.IR HOST ,
it is a stored
.IR HOST_LABEL ,
not an SSH host name.
.TP
.B trunk id
Print the mounted trunk device's identifier, read from
.IR TRUNK_MOUNT_POINT /.btrctl/trunk\-id.
This is the
.I ID
in
.BI trunk_ ID
tracking links.
.TP
.B trunk backup \fR[\fISET_NAME\fR] [\fB\-H\fR|\fB\-\-host\fR \fIHOST\fR]
Send
.IR SET_NAME ,
or the set
.B latest
points to when omitted, to the trunk.
With
.BR \-\-host ,
the set is read from
.I HOST
over SSH and received locally.
.IP
If
.IR TRUNK_MOUNT_POINT /@ HOST_LABEL
does not exist it is created as a new subvolume with a
.B .btrctl
marker file.
If it exists but is not a subvolume, or lacks the marker, the backup is
refused; to adopt a pre-existing subvolume, create an empty
.B .btrctl
file inside it.
A backup is also refused if the destination set already exists.
.IP
When the source's
.BI trunk_ ID
link points at an older set that still exists on both the source and the
trunk, that set is used as the parent for an incremental send; otherwise a
full send is used.
After a successful backup the set's
.B .btrctl
metadata is copied (regular files and directories only; files become mode 0644
and directories 0755, owned by root, with fresh timestamps) and the source's
.BI trunk_ ID
link is updated.
.IP
Values obtained from a remote host, namely its configuration and the set names
its
.B latest
and
.BI trunk_ ID
links resolve to, are validated before anything is created on the trunk; see
.BR \%CONFIGURATION .
.TP
.B trunk hosts
Print the
.I HOST_LABEL
of every btrctl-managed host subvolume on the trunk, one per line.
Subvolumes without a
.B .btrctl
marker are skipped.
.TP
.B trunk list \fR[\fIHOST\fR]
List the sets stored on the trunk, for every managed host or for
.I HOST
only.
Naming a
.I HOST
whose subvolume lacks the marker is an error.
.TP
.B trunk prune \fR{\fIHOST\fR|\fB\-a\fR|\fB\-\-all\fR} [\fB\-n\fR|\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
Apply the retention policy to one host's sets on the trunk, or to every
managed host with
.BR \-\-all .
Exactly one of the two must be given.
Retention is evaluated independently per host using the trunk machine's
clock.
Sets tagged
.B noprune
are kept; there are no
.B latest
or
.BI trunk_ ID
protections on the trunk.
Confirmation and
.B \-\-dry\-run
behave as for
.BR prune .
.TP
.B trunk rm \fIHOST\fB/\fISET_NAME
Delete one set from the trunk after confirmation, including its
.B .btrctl
metadata.
.I HOST
is required; there is no local-machine default.
.TP
.B trunk tag \fIHOST\fB/\fISET_NAME\fR {\fBadd\fR|\fBrm\fR} \fITAG\fR...
Add or remove tags on a set stored on the trunk, with the same rules as
.BR tag .
Tags on the trunk and on the source are independent after the backup copies
them.
.
.SS Other
.TP
.B config
Print the effective configuration as
.IB KEY = VALUE
lines.
This is also what a remote host is asked for when
.B trunk backup \-\-host
needs its configuration.
.TP
.BR help ", " \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a summary of commands.
.
.SH HOOKS
Two optional executables under
.IR BTRFS_MOUNT_POINT /.btrctl
are run by
.BR snapshot .
Both run as root with
.I BTRFS_MOUNT_POINT
as their working directory and in their environment.
Because they run as root, hooks must meet the same trust requirements as
.B BTRCTL_CONF
overlays (see
.BR ENVIRONMENT ):
after resolving symlinks, the hook and every directory above it, including
.I BTRFS_MOUNT_POINT
itself, must be owned by root and not group- or world-writable.
Both hooks are checked before anything is snapshotted; an untrusted hook is a
hard error.
A trusted file that is not executable is skipped with a warning.
.TP
.B pre\-snapshot
Runs before any subvolume is snapshotted.
A non-zero exit aborts the command before anything is created.
.TP
.B post\-snapshot
Runs after the set and the
.B latest
link have been created, with the additional variable
.B SNAPSHOT_DIR
set to the absolute path of the new set.
Anything the hook wants carried along by
.B trunk backup
should be written under
.IR SNAPSHOT_DIR /.btrctl.
A non-zero exit makes btrctl exit with an error, but the set is left in place
and must be cleaned up by hand.
.
.SH RETENTION POLICY
.B prune
and
.B trunk prune
keep the union of:
.IP \(bu 3
every set from the last 24 hours (reason
.BR recent );
.IP \(bu
the newest set in each hour, for 72 hours
.RB ( hourly );
.IP \(bu
the newest set in each day, for 7 days
.RB ( daily );
.IP \(bu
the newest set in each Sunday-start week, for 4 weeks
.RB ( weekly );
.IP \(bu
the newest set in each calendar month, for 12 months
.RB ( monthly );
.IP \(bu
the newest set in each calendar year, forever
.RB ( yearly ).
.PP
Protected sets (those pointed to by
.BR latest ,
.BI trunk_ ID\fR,
or tagged
.BR \%noprune )
still count as the newest in their buckets, so protecting a set can cause an
unprotected neighbour to be pruned.
Everything else is deleted, whole sets at a time including their metadata.
.PP
Age and bucket membership come from the set's directory name alone.
Hours, days and weeks are measured as elapsed time from the moment the command
starts; the 12-month window ends at the same local date and time one year
earlier (February 29 clamps to February 28, and a time that falls in a
daylight-saving gap moves forward through it).
Each window includes its cutoff instant.
Sets dated in the future are kept with reason
.BR future .
Directories whose names are not valid timestamps are skipped with a warning.
.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Configuration is read from
.I /etc/btrctl.conf
if it exists, then from the file named by
.B BTRCTL_CONF
if set (see
.BR ENVIRONMENT ).
Both are sourced as shell scripts.
The variables are:
.TP
.B HOST_LABEL
Name used for this machine in snapshot names, on the trunk, and in listings.
Default: the output of
.BR hostname (1).
.TP
.B BTRFS_MOUNT_POINT
Mount point of the btrfs file system that holds the subvolumes to snapshot.
The
.B @snapshots
directory is created under it.
It must already be mounted when a command needs it.
Default:
.IR /btrfs .
.TP
.B SUBVOLUMES
Bash array of subvolume names, relative to
.IR BTRFS_MOUNT_POINT ,
to include in each set.
Default:
.BR (@) .
.TP
.B TRUNK_MOUNT_POINT
Mount point of the trunk device.
Mounting and unmounting it is not btrctl's job.
Default:
.IR /trunk .
.PP
Because these values are embedded in shell commands run over SSH and used to
build paths, they are restricted to a conservative character set and checked
before
.B snapshot
and
.B trunk backup
do any work.
The same checks are applied to configuration fetched from a remote host.
.TP
.B HOST_LABEL
Letters, digits,
.BR . ,
.BR _ ,
.BR \- .
.TP
.B SUBVOLUMES
Letters, digits,
.BR @ ,
.BR . ,
.BR _ ,
.BR + ,
.BR \- .
.TP
.B BTRFS_MOUNT_POINT
An absolute path made of letters, digits,
.BR @ ,
.BR . ,
.BR _ ,
.BR + ,
.BR / ,
.BR \- ,
with no
.B ..
component.
.PP
None of them may be empty,
.BR . ,
or
.BR .. ,
or begin with
.BR \- .
The trunk identifier follows the
.B HOST_LABEL
rules.
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BTRCTL_CONF
Path to an overlay configuration file, sourced after
.IR /etc/btrctl.conf .
Variables it sets override the base configuration; anything it leaves unset
is inherited.
This allows a second, independent btrfs pool on the same machine to have its
own
.BR HOST_LABEL ,
.B BTRFS_MOUNT_POINT
and
.B SUBVOLUMES
while sharing
.BR TRUNK_MOUNT_POINT .
The variable is carried across the automatic sudo re-execution.
If it is set and the file does not exist, btrctl exits with an error.
.IP
Because the overlay is sourced as root, it must come from a trusted location.
After resolving symlinks, the file must be a regular file owned by root and
not group- or world-writable, and every directory on its path must also be
owned by root and not group- or world-writable.
Sticky directories such as
.I /tmp
do not qualify.
Anything else is a hard error.
.I /etc/btrctl.d/
is a suitable home for overlays.
This check is what makes it safe to grant btrctl through a restricted
.BR sudoers (5)
rule that includes
.BR SETENV .
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set to any non-empty value, disables coloured output.
Colour is in any case only used when the relevant stream is a terminal.
.TP
.B SUDO_USER
Set by
.BR sudo (8).
When present, SSH connections for
.B \-\-host
are made as this user rather than as root, so the invoking user's keys and
.BR ssh_config (5)
are used.
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/btrctl.conf
Base configuration.
.TP
.I /etc/btrctl.d/
Suggested location for
.B BTRCTL_CONF
overlays.
.TP
.IR BTRFS_MOUNT_POINT /@snapshots/ TIMESTAMP /
A snapshot set.
.TP
.IR BTRFS_MOUNT_POINT /@snapshots/ TIMESTAMP /.btrctl/tags
The set's tags, one per line.
.TP
.IR BTRFS_MOUNT_POINT /@snapshots/latest
Symlink to the most recently created set.
.TP
.IR BTRFS_MOUNT_POINT /@snapshots/trunk_ ID
Symlink to the set most recently backed up to trunk
.IR ID .
.TP
.IR BTRFS_MOUNT_POINT /.btrctl/pre\-snapshot
.TQ
.IR BTRFS_MOUNT_POINT /.btrctl/post\-snapshot
Snapshot hooks; see
.BR HOOKS .
.TP
.IR TRUNK_MOUNT_POINT /.btrctl/trunk\-id
Identifier of the trunk device.
Create this file by hand when preparing a new trunk.
.TP
.IR TRUNK_MOUNT_POINT /@ HOST_LABEL /
A host's backups on the trunk.
Must contain an empty
.B .btrctl
marker file.
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on any error or when the user declines a confirmation.
Commands run with
.B \-\-host
exit with the remote command's status.
.
.SH EXAMPLES
Take a tagged snapshot set and list the result:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ btrctl snapshot \-\-tag pre\-upgrade
$ btrctl list
.fi
.RE
.PP
Preview what pruning would remove, then do it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ btrctl prune \-\-dry\-run
$ btrctl prune
.fi
.RE
.PP
Back up the latest set from a remote machine to the attached trunk, then
check what the trunk holds:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ btrctl trunk backup \-\-host db01
$ btrctl trunk list db01
.fi
.RE
.PP
Keep a set indefinitely:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ btrctl tag 2026\-10\-07\-120000 add noprune
.fi
.RE
.PP
Use the latest set's name in a script:
.PP
.RS
.nf
set=$(btrctl latest)
.fi
.RE
.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR btrfs (8),
.BR btrfs\-subvolume (8),
.BR btrfs\-send (8),
.BR btrfs\-receive (8),
.BR ssh (1),
.BR sudo (8)
